body:
Parent programme: #740
Structural phase theorem lane: #760 / PR #761
Full-stack semantic/resource context: ORION-QN #734; QG-10 #763

## Status

**FAULT-TOLERANT LIFT FRONTIER — transfer theorem or explicit nonlinearity/coupling failure.**

ORION-QG currently proves or predicts regimes under structural compiler objectives. Real fault-tolerant execution is governed by logical decomposition, QEC code distance, lattice-surgery geometry, magic-state factories, routing/congestion, measurement/feed-forward and hardware error/timing assumptions.

QG-11 asks:

> When can a proof-carrying structural compiler regime/normal-form phase be transported through a fault-tolerant resource model to yield a certified hardware/QEC regime boundary, and when do nonlinear stack effects invalidate that transport?

## Strong donor subtraction

This lane gives zero novelty credit to full-stack QRE, FT compilers or hardware-aware multi-objective optimization:

- **AutoQuREO** (2026) already provides automated full-stack QRE/optimization with configurable stack components, surrogate models and multi-objective design-space exploration;
- **Microsoft Quantum Resource Estimator** already exposes logical/physical resource models, T-state/T-factory counts, physical qubits, runtime and space-time Pareto frontiers, including custom factories/QEC/hardware;
- **Harvest** (2026) co-optimizes lattice-surgery placement/routing/scheduling with protocol-agnostic magic-state supply;
- compilation-driven QRE already translates logical primitives into configurable physical-resource costs;
- lattice-surgery scheduling/resource-aware FT compilation is established prior art.

Therefore `resource estimation`, `T-factory modeling`, `Pareto hardware optimization`, `lattice-surgery scheduling`, and `hardware-aware compilation` are donor-owned.

Candidate contribution is only the **proof-carrying transfer of ORION-QG regime geometry across resource layers**, including exact conditions under which a structural phase theorem remains valid and explicit CANNOT_CHECK/nonlinear bifurcation when it does not.

## Two resource layers

### Layer S — structural compiler coordinates

For a TARE/R6M example, a configuration carries a structural vector such as

`r_S = (U_nc, U_c, TagSupport, F3Restore, Rotations, logical_qubits, measurements, ...)`.

QG-8 reasons over linear scalarizations `theta · r_S` and proves a support-two normal-form cone for particular theta half-spaces.

### Layer F — FT stack coordinates

A frozen FT model maps verified structural witnesses to

`r_F = (T_count, T_depth, Clifford_count, logical_qubits, logical_depth, measurements, feedforward_rounds, physical_qubits, factory_qubits, factory_copies, QEC_cycles, runtime, space_time_volume, failure_budget, ...)`.

The mapping must carry exact stack identity:
- logical primitive library/version;
- compiler/lowering identity;
- QEC code/model;
- physical error model;
- cycle/gate/measurement times;
- T-factory/cultivation protocol;
- layout/routing model;
- target algorithm error budget.

## F1 — affine-transfer theorem

First study a deliberately bounded model where FT cost is an affine/monotone function of the structural vector:

`r_F = A r_S + b`,

and final scalar objective is `lambda · r_F`.

Then the induced structural coefficient vector is

`theta = A^T lambda`.

If QG-8 certifies `theta` inside a structural phase cone, the normal-form theorem transfers mechanically.

Positive theorem target:

> Under an explicitly affine, witness-independent FT resource map A and monotone scalar objective lambda, every structural ORION-QG phase cell pulls back to a polyhedral FT objective/hardware cell via `A^T lambda`; theorem ownership and claim scope are preserved.

This linear-algebra statement itself is elementary; novelty, if any, is the certified compiler/FT instantiation and anti-laundering transfer protocol.

## F2 — piecewise-affine stack effects

Real resource models contain discrete changes:
- code distance changes with error budget/runtime;
- integer T-factory copy counts;
- distillation protocol changes;
- routing/layout changes;
- parallelism and congestion saturation;
- measurement/feed-forward critical paths.

Freeze a stack model and partition it into cells in which the resource map is affine. Within each cell apply F1. Boundaries become **joint compiler/QEC phase surfaces**.

Required output per cell:
- exact stack assumptions;
- affine resource-map certificate or verified local formula;
- induced theta;
- ORION-QG structural phase;
- resulting physical resource expression;
- boundary predicates.

## F3 — nonseparable counterexamples

Actively search for cases where two structural configurations with the same ORION-QG resource vector have different physical costs because of layout/schedule structure. Any such witness refutes vector sufficiency and forces the structural state to be enriched.

Candidate nonlinear mechanisms:
- two circuits with equal T-count/T-depth but different T-factory delivery congestion;
- equal support/rotation count but different lattice-surgery routing;
- same logical depth but different measurement/feed-forward critical path;
- factory integer rounding causing a regime flip not predicted by linear theta;
- code-distance changes caused by different runtime/error allocation.

Positive discovery terminal:
`QG11_STRUCTURAL_VECTOR_INSUFFICIENT__NEW_FT_COUPLING_COORDINATE_REQUIRED`

This is as valuable as a successful transfer because it identifies the missing physical coordinate.

## F4 — real estimator integration

After analytic calibration, use current Microsoft QRE or an equivalently frozen open resource estimator to evaluate the exact same verified ORION-QG witnesses under multiple hardware/QEC stacks.

Do **not** use estimator output as proof of a structural theorem. Its role is:
- instantiate/model physical coordinates;
- locate candidate piecewise cells/crossovers;
- validate analytic transfer predictions where estimator semantics match;
- expose hidden coordinates where predictions fail.

Required frozen stacks should include at least two materially different regimes (e.g. generic surface-code-like stack and a different gate/error/timing/factory configuration). Custom model identity/digests are receipts.

## F5 — hardware evolution forecast

Once a cell decomposition is frozen, select an unseen hardware/QEC parameter point, predict the preferred ORION-QG normal form and projected resource ordering **before** running the full estimator/compiler. Then reveal the estimator result.

This is the FT analogue of QG-3/QG-5 prospective regime forecasting.

## Metrics

- regime-transfer correctness;
- false physical-regime promotion rate;
- CANNOT_CHECK calibration;
- fraction of parameter space covered by certified affine/piecewise cells;
- physical resource prediction error within cells;
- number/type of discovered missing physical coordinates;
- stability of boundaries under reasonable stack uncertainty;
- cross-estimator reproducibility where two estimators can model the same stack.

## Dual harness

Generic ORION harness:
- validates resource-map equations and stack identities;
- independently recomputes induced theta/cell membership;
- reconstructs estimator receipts.

Native ORION-Q harness:
- keeps `STRUCTURAL_THEOREM`, `FT_TRANSFER`, and `PHYSICAL_ESTIMATE` as distinct authority coordinates;
- refuses transfer when resource map is nonlinear/unresolved;
- reopens a physical regime receipt when stack/compiler/QEC identity changes.

## Success terminals

- `QG11_AFFINE_FT_REGIME_TRANSFER_MACHINE_CHECKED`
- `QG11_PIECEWISE_FT_PHASE_DIAGRAM_SUPPORTED`
- `QG11_PROSPECTIVE_FT_REGIME_FORECAST_CONFIRMED`
- `QG11_STRUCTURAL_VECTOR_INSUFFICIENT__NEW_FT_COUPLING_COORDINATE_REQUIRED`
- `QG11_ESTIMATOR_SEMANTICS_CANNOT_CHECK`
- `QG11_DONOR_PARENT_FOUND`

## Claim boundary

This lane does not claim real-QPU speedup. A resource estimator yields projected physical resources under its stack assumptions, not observed hardware advantage. Any end-to-end quantum advantage remains separately gated by ORION-QN access/state-preparation/baseline rules.
